WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.891 --> 00:00:15.291
In Search of Dharma.

2
00:00:16.794 --> 00:00:18.751
4: A World of Dharmas.

3
00:00:20.931 --> 00:00:23.951
A relational web, a rational fortress,

4
00:00:24.051 --> 00:00:27.611
a sung land, an open hand, a commanding
covenant:

5
00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:32.871
five ways of living that disagree about
almost everything, and rhyme underneath.

6
00:00:33.271 --> 00:00:36.771
Including, if you have the nerve to look,
your own.

7
00:00:38.691 --> 00:00:41.131
Picture five people, in five worlds,

8
00:00:41.131 --> 00:00:44.511
each answering the same question: how
should a person live?

9
00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:50.191
An elder in a southern African village is
asked what makes a good person,

10
00:00:50.271 --> 00:00:52.611
and she does not start with the person at
all.

11
00:00:52.871 --> 00:00:54.831
She starts with everyone else.

12
00:00:55.331 --> 00:00:59.791
A human being, she says, becomes human
only through other human beings;

13
00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:04.411
you are a person because, and only
because, you belong to a people.

14
00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:10.371
A Roman emperor sits in a campaign tent on
a freezing frontier,

15
00:01:10.471 --> 00:01:12.531
writing to himself by lamplight,

16
00:01:12.591 --> 00:01:16.991
reminding himself that the empire he
commands, the body he inhabits,

17
00:01:17.171 --> 00:01:21.091
and the very next breath he takes are not
finally his to keep,

18
00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:23.531
and that the only thing that is truly his,

19
00:01:23.531 --> 00:01:27.351
the only thing worth guarding, is the
quality of his own choices.

20
00:01:29.191 --> 00:01:33.791
An old man in the Australian desert walks
a path his ancestors walked,

21
00:01:33.891 --> 00:01:35.231
singing as he goes,

22
00:01:35.251 --> 00:01:39.371
because the song is the map and the law
and the history all at once,

23
00:01:39.751 --> 00:01:42.991
and the land beneath his feet is not
scenery but scripture,

24
00:01:43.291 --> 00:01:45.851
sung into being and sung into order.

25
00:01:47.391 --> 00:01:49.311
In a court city on Java,

26
00:01:49.311 --> 00:01:52.931
a bank clerk sits at home in the evening
with his eyes closed,

27
00:01:52.931 --> 00:01:55.071
doing what looks like nothing at all:

28
00:01:55.151 --> 00:01:59.671
practising the hardest discipline he
knows, the letting go of discipline itself,

29
00:01:59.751 --> 00:02:03.051
releasing opinion, effort, and will so
that,

30
00:02:03.051 --> 00:02:05.811
as he would put it, God may act through
him.

31
00:02:07.551 --> 00:02:11.111
And somewhere in the ancient Near East, a
person sets out,

32
00:02:11.111 --> 00:02:14.671
quite deliberately, to walk a way: to live
every hour,

33
00:02:14.671 --> 00:02:17.551
the meals and the washing and the buying
and the burying,

34
00:02:17.651 --> 00:02:21.731
according to a path laid down in a
covenant between a people and their God.

35
00:02:23.374 --> 00:02:24.511
Five answers.

36
00:02:24.971 --> 00:02:26.371
They look nothing alike.

37
00:02:26.931 --> 00:02:29.491
The first dissolves the self into the
community;

38
00:02:29.491 --> 00:02:31.551
the second builds a fortress around it.

39
00:02:32.271 --> 00:02:34.571
The third writes the law into the ground;

40
00:02:34.791 --> 00:02:40.511
the fourth hands the self over to its God;
the fifth receives the law from the sky.

41
00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:42.251
And yet they rhyme.

42
00:02:42.571 --> 00:02:45.111
What follows is a tour of that
strangeness,

43
00:02:45.111 --> 00:02:47.831
the irreducible variety of human dharmas,

44
00:02:48.051 --> 00:02:51.351
and the stubborn family likeness that runs
underneath them all.

45
00:02:54.291 --> 00:02:55.831
How far can they get?

46
00:02:58.051 --> 00:03:02.411
The wong sikep of the last part were one
dharma, watched up close.

47
00:03:02.951 --> 00:03:05.091
Now pull back and take in the range,

48
00:03:05.191 --> 00:03:08.231
because the plurality I keep insisting on
is a real

49
00:03:08.231 --> 00:03:11.591
and sometimes dizzying fact about the
human world.

50
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:16.251
So we are going to visit five dharmas that
grew up far apart,

51
00:03:16.251 --> 00:03:18.091
look at each on its own terms,

52
00:03:18.131 --> 00:03:21.751
and only then ask what, if anything, holds
across them.

53
00:03:22.291 --> 00:03:27.031
One thread runs through every stop: where
each dharma draws its circle of care,

54
00:03:27.071 --> 00:03:29.811
who it gathers in and who it leaves
outside.

55
00:03:31.751 --> 00:03:35.771
I am going to end the tour with the one
many of you were raised inside,

56
00:03:35.831 --> 00:03:40.671
the family of Jewish, Christian, and
Muslim ethics, and I should say why.

57
00:03:40.891 --> 00:03:43.351
The whole wager here is that we can look
at dharmas

58
00:03:43.351 --> 00:03:46.451
without privileging any of them, including
our own.

59
00:03:46.971 --> 00:03:50.171
That is easy to say about somebody else's
tradition and hard

60
00:03:50.171 --> 00:03:53.131
to do about the water you were swimming in
before you could see it.

61
00:03:53.751 --> 00:03:56.031
So the Abrahamic covenant comes last,

62
00:03:56.351 --> 00:03:58.531
as the real test of whether the
non-privileging

63
00:03:58.531 --> 00:04:00.751
eye can be turned around to look at
itself.

64
00:04:02.571 --> 00:04:04.651
And one tradition needs different
handling,

65
00:04:04.651 --> 00:04:07.591
so let me do it here at the door, before
the tour proper.

66
00:04:08.112 --> 00:04:11.771
Buddhism, the tradition through which the
word dharma reached most of us,

67
00:04:11.771 --> 00:04:13.451
is this project's home water:

68
00:04:13.451 --> 00:04:17.091
the one I look with more than at, the
source of half my instruments,

69
00:04:17.251 --> 00:04:20.391
and, as the Preface disclosed, the name on
this book's cover.

70
00:04:21.168 --> 00:04:24.511
I cannot cross-examine it with the
detachment I am asking of you,

71
00:04:24.511 --> 00:04:26.651
and for most of this project's life I used

72
00:04:26.651 --> 00:04:28.871
that as a reason to excuse it from the
stand.

73
00:04:29.451 --> 00:04:33.691
That was a mistake, or at least a
fingerprint pretending to be a courtesy.

74
00:04:34.311 --> 00:04:37.131
Partiality disclosed is not exemption
earned.

75
00:04:37.566 --> 00:04:40.271
So let it take the witness chair like
everyone else,

76
00:04:40.311 --> 00:04:42.731
briefly, with my hands where you can watch
them.

77
00:04:44.431 --> 00:04:46.671
As a dharma it is the textbook case;

78
00:04:47.011 --> 00:04:51.571
Part 1 already scored its four features
full marks, and no one contests them.

79
00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:55.271
The real questions are the ones this book
is building toward:

80
00:04:55.471 --> 00:04:58.731
does it hold without crushing, and who may
revise it?

81
00:04:59.291 --> 00:05:02.111
On holding, twenty-five centuries at every
scale

82
00:05:02.111 --> 00:05:06.051
from forest hermitage to imperial church
speak for themselves.

83
00:05:06.771 --> 00:05:10.451
On crushing, the record is there like
every other great tradition's.

84
00:05:11.231 --> 00:05:14.651
The Vinaya's own account has the founder
admitting women to ordination

85
00:05:14.891 --> 00:05:19.491
while attaching eight "heavy rules" that
subordinate every nun, however senior,

86
00:05:19.491 --> 00:05:21.451
to any monk, however junior;

87
00:05:21.871 --> 00:05:24.131
the women's ordination lineage was then
allowed

88
00:05:24.131 --> 00:05:27.511
to die out across the Theravada world for
most of a millennium,

89
00:05:27.511 --> 00:05:29.391
and its modern restoration is still being

90
00:05:29.391 --> 00:05:32.111
fought over by hierarchies that hold the
keys;

91
00:05:32.451 --> 00:05:34.671
and when the holding turned
ethno-national,

92
00:05:34.691 --> 00:05:37.191
it produced the preaching monk Part 6 will
meet.

93
00:05:39.411 --> 00:05:42.171
On revision, the founding archive contains
one

94
00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:44.811
of the most instructive scenes in any
tradition:

95
00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:48.511
on his deathbed the Buddha explicitly
permitted the sangha

96
00:05:48.511 --> 00:05:51.191
to abolish the lesser and minor rules
after him,

97
00:05:51.471 --> 00:05:54.431
a revision hatch built in by the founder
himself.

98
00:05:55.111 --> 00:05:58.291
At the First Council, months later, the
elders,

99
00:05:58.331 --> 00:06:03.091
unsure which rules counted as lesser,
resolved the doubt by abolishing none of them.

100
00:06:04.305 --> 00:06:07.231
The hatch was welded almost before the
body was cold,

101
00:06:07.231 --> 00:06:10.651
and the great revisions since have mostly
come from the margins or the exits:

102
00:06:11.171 --> 00:06:14.291
the half-million who will walk out with
Ambedkar in Part 6,

103
00:06:14.491 --> 00:06:17.391
the modernist remaking Part 8 returns to,

104
00:06:17.471 --> 00:06:20.351
Batchelor's secular reconstruction out at
the far edge.

105
00:06:22.111 --> 00:06:25.931
So examined, Buddhism stands where the
others on this tour will stand:

106
00:06:25.931 --> 00:06:29.371
a magnificent holding-system with its
pauses on the record,

107
00:06:29.371 --> 00:06:31.431
at the women and at the welded hatch.

108
00:06:32.431 --> 00:06:34.911
And one fingerprint, stated plainly:

109
00:06:34.911 --> 00:06:37.251
the secular dharma this book works in
descends

110
00:06:37.251 --> 00:06:40.091
from exactly the revisable margin of this
tradition,

111
00:06:40.091 --> 00:06:43.431
which is surely part of why this book
prizes revisability.

112
00:06:43.871 --> 00:06:46.911
A lens ground at the margin will find the
margin admirable.

113
00:06:47.471 --> 00:06:50.591
Now you know that too, and can discount
accordingly.

114
00:06:53.416 --> 00:06:55.651
Ubuntu: the self is a "we"

115
00:06:57.769 --> 00:06:59.471
Start in southern Africa,

116
00:06:59.611 --> 00:07:04.771
with a single sentence in the Nguni
languages: umuntu ngumuntu ngabantu.

117
00:07:05.271 --> 00:07:07.891
"A person is a person through other
persons."

118
00:07:08.391 --> 00:07:12.871
In its travelling English form it became
"I am because we are," and behind

119
00:07:12.871 --> 00:07:16.891
that gentle slogan sits one of the most
radical claims any dharma makes,

120
00:07:17.011 --> 00:07:21.211
a claim that reaches past how you should
behave to what you fundamentally are.

121
00:07:22.891 --> 00:07:27.311
In the world most of my readers grew up
in, the individual comes first.

122
00:07:27.811 --> 00:07:30.511
You are a self, complete and bounded,

123
00:07:30.511 --> 00:07:35.151
and then you enter into relationships,
take on duties, join groups.

124
00:07:35.831 --> 00:07:39.931
Descartes gave that world its motto: I
think, therefore I am.

125
00:07:40.431 --> 00:07:43.011
The lone mind, alone, is the bedrock.

126
00:07:43.451 --> 00:07:45.551
Ubuntu turns this inside out.

127
00:07:45.951 --> 00:07:50.531
The proverb is Nguni; the argument it
carries runs the length of the continent.

128
00:07:51.311 --> 00:07:55.371
On the account given by the Nigerian
philosopher Ifeanyi Menkiti,

129
00:07:55.431 --> 00:07:57.771
personhood is not something you are issued
at birth;

130
00:07:57.811 --> 00:07:59.331
it is something you achieve,

131
00:07:59.331 --> 00:08:02.071
and you achieve it only by taking your
place in a community

132
00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:03.911
and discharging what you owe it.

133
00:08:04.271 --> 00:08:05.211
You can be a living,

134
00:08:05.211 --> 00:08:08.971
breathing member of the species and still
not yet be, in the full sense,

135
00:08:09.011 --> 00:08:11.991
a person, because personhood is a moral
accomplishment,

136
00:08:12.251 --> 00:08:14.151
grown in the soil of relationship.

137
00:08:14.551 --> 00:08:16.491
The self grows out of the "we."

138
00:08:17.758 --> 00:08:21.571
It is tempting to hear in this the simple
erasure of the individual,

139
00:08:21.611 --> 00:08:24.251
the single person swallowed whole by the
group,

140
00:08:24.311 --> 00:08:27.391
and Ubuntu's own thinkers are alert to the
misreading.

141
00:08:27.811 --> 00:08:31.011
One of them, the Ghanaian philosopher
Kwame Gyekye,

142
00:08:31.051 --> 00:08:33.451
pushed back against the most radical
version,

143
00:08:33.451 --> 00:08:36.991
the one that would make the community the
entire substance of the person.

144
00:08:37.471 --> 00:08:42.171
A fully realised human being, he insisted,
is also a chooser and a reasoner,

145
00:08:42.251 --> 00:08:44.251
a bearer of conscience who can

146
00:08:44.271 --> 00:08:48.051
and sometimes must stand against the group
when the group has gone wrong.

147
00:08:48.431 --> 00:08:51.411
Ubuntu at its best does not abolish the
individual;

148
00:08:51.571 --> 00:08:54.071
it denies that the individual comes first.

149
00:08:55.351 --> 00:08:57.871
There is a real difference between saying
you are nothing

150
00:08:57.871 --> 00:09:01.351
but your relationships and saying you
become yourself through them,

151
00:09:01.471 --> 00:09:04.131
and the more careful readings of Ubuntu
mean the second.

152
00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:10.711
In the hands of Thaddeus Metz, a
philosopher working in South Africa,

153
00:09:10.731 --> 00:09:13.731
Ubuntu hardens into a precise moral
theory:

154
00:09:13.891 --> 00:09:17.531
an action is right insofar as it honours
relationships in

155
00:09:17.531 --> 00:09:21.631
which people recognise themselves in one
another and act in solidarity.

156
00:09:22.231 --> 00:09:24.371
And it has done real public work.

157
00:09:24.991 --> 00:09:28.111
When apartheid ended and South Africa had
to decide what

158
00:09:28.111 --> 00:09:31.031
to do with the torturers and killers of
the old regime,

159
00:09:31.031 --> 00:09:34.111
it did not reach mainly for the courtroom
and the prison.

160
00:09:34.551 --> 00:09:38.751
Under Desmond Tutu, it established a Truth
and Reconciliation Commission,

161
00:09:38.751 --> 00:09:41.751
an attempt to heal a fractured community
by bringing the truth

162
00:09:41.751 --> 00:09:45.611
to light and reintegrating even
perpetrators into the moral world,

163
00:09:45.611 --> 00:09:47.871
rather than simply exacting retribution.

164
00:09:48.411 --> 00:09:51.871
And when the new Constitutional Court
struck down the death penalty,

165
00:09:51.871 --> 00:09:54.331
it did so partly in the name of Ubuntu,

166
00:09:54.351 --> 00:09:55.111
holding that a state

167
00:09:55.111 --> 00:09:58.731
which kills its own betrays the
recognition of shared humanity on

168
00:09:58.911 --> 00:10:00.771
which a decent society rests.

169
00:10:01.231 --> 00:10:04.771
A proverb became a principle of
constitutional law.

170
00:10:06.451 --> 00:10:11.051
That sentence needs one qualification,
because the timing is stranger than it looks.

171
00:10:11.431 --> 00:10:12.951
The proverb is old.

172
00:10:13.612 --> 00:10:16.791
Its use as the definition of ubuntu
appears to be recent:

173
00:10:17.091 --> 00:10:19.591
the historian of ideas Christian Gade,

174
00:10:19.731 --> 00:10:22.311
tracing when people actually wrote the two
together,

175
00:10:22.351 --> 00:10:25.411
puts it between 1993 and 1995,

176
00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.771
the very years of the interim constitution

177
00:10:28.031 --> 00:10:30.931
and the founding of the Truth and
Reconciliation Commission.

178
00:10:31.911 --> 00:10:34.931
He offers that as a hypothesis about the
written record rather

179
00:10:35.291 --> 00:10:37.391
than a claim about what villages said,

180
00:10:37.391 --> 00:10:39.691
and it does not make ubuntu an invention.

181
00:10:40.291 --> 00:10:42.571
But it does mean the warm ancestral
picture

182
00:10:42.571 --> 00:10:46.111
and the constitutional moment arrived more
or less hand in hand,

183
00:10:46.151 --> 00:10:48.051
each lending the other authority.

184
00:10:48.471 --> 00:10:53.151
A dharma being consolidated exactly when a
country needed one is not a scandal.

185
00:10:53.511 --> 00:10:58.571
It is my whole argument about how dharmas
get made: as a response to externalities.

186
00:11:00.354 --> 00:11:03.811
I have to add the shadow, because every
dharma on this tour has one,

187
00:11:04.031 --> 00:11:05.551
and Ubuntu's is instructive.

188
00:11:06.571 --> 00:11:09.491
The warm picture of timeless African
togetherness is,

189
00:11:09.511 --> 00:11:13.791
in part, a story told later, and it can be
put to ugly uses:

190
00:11:13.991 --> 00:11:16.931
a demand for "harmony" can be a demand for
silence,

191
00:11:17.091 --> 00:11:20.411
a way of shaming the dissenter and
protecting the powerful.

192
00:11:20.824 --> 00:11:23.031
And there is a bitter irony on the record.

193
00:11:23.211 --> 00:11:26.351
The same post-apartheid South Africa that
wrote Ubuntu

194
00:11:26.351 --> 00:11:30.571
into its highest law has seen waves of
murderous violence against migrants

195
00:11:30.571 --> 00:11:32.271
from other African countries,

196
00:11:32.311 --> 00:11:33.251
the supposed brothers

197
00:11:33.251 --> 00:11:36.811
and sisters of the human family attacked
as unwanted strangers.

198
00:11:37.351 --> 00:11:39.551
The web that includes can also exclude.

199
00:11:39.951 --> 00:11:43.391
We have met this before, the hard edge
around the warm circle,

200
00:11:43.411 --> 00:11:44.791
and we will meet it again.

201
00:11:47.727 --> 00:11:49.511
The Stoics: the fortress inside

202
00:11:52.991 --> 00:11:55.891
Now travel back, to the Greek and Roman
world,

203
00:11:55.891 --> 00:11:58.251
for a dharma that will feel oddly modern,

204
00:11:58.311 --> 00:12:01.911
because it is one of the most nearly
secular the ancient world produced.

205
00:12:03.711 --> 00:12:07.611
Two of its greatest teachers could hardly
have been further apart in station.

206
00:12:08.411 --> 00:12:12.071
Epictetus was born a slave, and taught,
after he was freed,

207
00:12:12.071 --> 00:12:14.831
that no master could touch the one thing
that mattered.

208
00:12:15.531 --> 00:12:20.071
Marcus Aurelius was the most powerful man
in the Roman world, its emperor,

209
00:12:20.111 --> 00:12:24.111
and spent his private nights reminding
himself of exactly the same thing.

210
00:12:24.631 --> 00:12:28.211
That a slave and an emperor could practise
one and the same discipline,

211
00:12:28.211 --> 00:12:32.531
and arrive at one and the same freedom,
tells you most of what Stoicism is.

212
00:12:34.431 --> 00:12:35.791
Its core is a single,

213
00:12:35.791 --> 00:12:39.371
bracing distinction that Epictetus put at
the very front of his handbook:

214
00:12:39.451 --> 00:12:42.131
some things are up to us, and some things
are not.

215
00:12:42.691 --> 00:12:46.591
Our opinions, our intentions, our own
choices and responses are ours.

216
00:12:47.351 --> 00:12:51.951
Almost everything else -- our bodies, our
reputations, our wealth,

217
00:12:52.071 --> 00:12:56.971
the behaviour of other people, the turn of
events -- is not finally in our control.

218
00:12:57.831 --> 00:13:00.191
The whole of wisdom, the Stoics taught,

219
00:13:00.191 --> 00:13:03.591
is to draw that line clearly and then to
invest your hopes

220
00:13:03.591 --> 00:13:06.771
and your sense of yourself only on the
inner side of it.

221
00:13:07.311 --> 00:13:11.151
Want what is up to you, accept what is
not, and no tyrant,

222
00:13:11.151 --> 00:13:14.851
no accident, no loss can reach the citadel
of your character.

223
00:13:15.471 --> 00:13:20.051
Virtue, the excellence of your own
choosing self, is the only true good;

224
00:13:20.191 --> 00:13:24.711
everything else is, at most, raw material
to be used well or badly.

225
00:13:26.431 --> 00:13:30.811
Underneath the ethics sat a picture of the
cosmos as fundamentally rational,

226
00:13:30.891 --> 00:13:33.491
ordered by what the Greeks called the
logos,

227
00:13:33.591 --> 00:13:35.631
a reason running through all things,

228
00:13:35.731 --> 00:13:39.491
which a human being could align with by
living "according to nature."

229
00:13:39.491 --> 00:13:42.531
But that metaphysics sits lightly on the
practice.

230
00:13:42.891 --> 00:13:45.251
You do not have to believe in a particular
god

231
00:13:45.251 --> 00:13:47.911
to draw Epictetus's line between the
controllable

232
00:13:47.911 --> 00:13:51.331
and the uncontrollable and to reorder your
life around it.

233
00:13:51.951 --> 00:13:54.531
Which is exactly why Stoicism has come
roaring

234
00:13:54.531 --> 00:13:57.371
back in our own anxious, secular century,

235
00:13:57.391 --> 00:13:59.451
repackaged in a thousand books and apps

236
00:13:59.451 --> 00:14:02.091
for people who would never set foot in a
temple.

237
00:14:02.511 --> 00:14:06.291
It is a dharma that travels with
remarkably little metaphysical luggage,

238
00:14:06.471 --> 00:14:08.451
a portable fortress for the self.

239
00:14:10.254 --> 00:14:13.091
I should say that the Stoics themselves
would dispute that,

240
00:14:13.091 --> 00:14:15.311
and so do many of their best modern
readers.

241
00:14:16.111 --> 00:14:19.171
For Epictetus the cosmos is providentially
ordered,

242
00:14:19.251 --> 00:14:21.951
and "living according to nature" means
aligning

243
00:14:21.951 --> 00:14:24.391
with a rational plan that is actually
there;

244
00:14:24.671 --> 00:14:28.791
scholars like AA Long argue that the
theology is not decoration but load-bearing,

245
00:14:29.491 --> 00:14:31.911
the thing that makes the resignation
reasonable rather

246
00:14:31.991 --> 00:14:34.611
than merely stoical in the small-letter
sense.

247
00:14:35.531 --> 00:14:38.851
Accept the universe when it is governed by
reason and you are trusting;

248
00:14:39.311 --> 00:14:42.431
accept it when it is governed by nothing
and you are just bracing.

249
00:14:43.227 --> 00:14:46.811
So the better description is not that
Stoicism came without luggage.

250
00:14:47.771 --> 00:14:51.491
It is that the luggage can be, and is
being, left behind,

251
00:14:51.491 --> 00:14:55.631
and that the practice keeps working well
enough for millions of people to carry on.

252
00:14:55.871 --> 00:14:57.931
Keep that in view, because we are about

253
00:14:57.931 --> 00:15:02.611
to meet a Javanese fellowship doing the
very same operation on its own idea of God,

254
00:15:02.611 --> 00:15:05.651
and it will raise the question this whole
book is walking toward:

255
00:15:05.651 --> 00:15:09.891
how much of a dharma's metaphysics can you
unscrew before the thing stops holding?

256
00:15:11.611 --> 00:15:13.411
And the fortress points outward,

257
00:15:13.411 --> 00:15:16.871
in a way you might not expect from a
philosophy of the inner citadel.

258
00:15:17.551 --> 00:15:19.491
If reason runs through all things,

259
00:15:19.491 --> 00:15:22.271
then it runs through all people, and every
human being,

260
00:15:22.271 --> 00:15:26.451
slave or emperor, shares in the same logos
and the same fundamental worth.

261
00:15:27.096 --> 00:15:31.751
The Stoics drew the obvious conclusion and
called themselves citizens of the world,

262
00:15:31.911 --> 00:15:34.531
members of a single community of rational
beings

263
00:15:34.531 --> 00:15:37.271
that takes no account of city, tribe, or
birth.

264
00:15:37.979 --> 00:15:40.911
Marcus, ruling an empire built on
conquest,

265
00:15:40.951 --> 00:15:42.471
reminded himself nonetheless

266
00:15:42.471 --> 00:15:45.931
that he owed the foreigner the same basic
regard he owed his own kin.

267
00:15:46.971 --> 00:15:50.451
Set beside the others on this tour, the
contrast is sharp:

268
00:15:50.571 --> 00:15:54.751
where Ubuntu binds you tightly to a
particular people and the Dreaming,

269
00:15:54.751 --> 00:15:57.271
our next stop, to a particular country,

270
00:15:57.391 --> 00:16:00.871
the Stoic stretches the circle to its
widest possible edge,

271
00:16:00.911 --> 00:16:02.731
the whole of humanity at once.

272
00:16:03.271 --> 00:16:05.771
The same impulse to draw a circle of care,

273
00:16:05.811 --> 00:16:08.711
and wildly different ideas about where to
draw it.

274
00:16:10.431 --> 00:16:12.611
It has its own failure mode, of course.

275
00:16:13.351 --> 00:16:15.111
Push the acceptance of "what is not up

276
00:16:15.111 --> 00:16:18.091
to us" too far and the fortress becomes a
retreat:

277
00:16:18.131 --> 00:16:19.991
a counsel of passivity that can shade

278
00:16:19.991 --> 00:16:23.731
into indifference toward injustice you
might actually be able to change.

279
00:16:24.531 --> 00:16:29.051
And one question presses on any philosophy
that says externals do not matter:

280
00:16:29.111 --> 00:16:33.071
it is a good deal easier to believe that
wealth and status are indifferent when,

281
00:16:33.071 --> 00:16:35.711
like Marcus, you already have all of both.

282
00:16:36.271 --> 00:16:40.651
A dharma can be true and still be more
comfortable for some to hold than others.

283
00:16:43.511 --> 00:16:45.571
The Dreaming: the law in the land

284
00:16:48.011 --> 00:16:49.511
Now go to Australia,

285
00:16:49.551 --> 00:16:54.771
and to dharmas so old and so unlike the
others that they stretch the very category:

286
00:16:54.851 --> 00:16:58.071
the hundreds of distinct Aboriginal
traditions the English

287
00:16:58.071 --> 00:17:01.891
language clumsily gathers under a single
word, the Dreaming.

288
00:17:03.591 --> 00:17:05.731
Begin by throwing away three things you

289
00:17:05.731 --> 00:17:08.251
might think a serious ethical tradition
needs.

290
00:17:08.791 --> 00:17:11.831
There is no scripture, because there is no
writing.

291
00:17:12.171 --> 00:17:16.751
There is no founder, no Buddha, Christ, or
Confucius at the origin.

292
00:17:17.291 --> 00:17:22.011
And there is no separate "code," no list
of commandments you could copy onto a page.

293
00:17:22.661 --> 00:17:24.891
Instead, there is the Tjukurrpa,

294
00:17:24.911 --> 00:17:28.551
to give it its Western Desert name, for
each language has its own:

295
00:17:28.551 --> 00:17:32.191
a word that means the ancestral past, the
present reality,

296
00:17:32.191 --> 00:17:35.731
the moral law, and the structure of the
world, all at once.

297
00:17:36.851 --> 00:17:41.431
The anthropologist WEH Stanner, seeking a
way to translate it,

298
00:17:41.431 --> 00:17:45.491
coined the word "everywhen": a time that
is not back then but always,

299
00:17:45.491 --> 00:17:47.491
in which the acts of the ancestral beings

300
00:17:47.491 --> 00:17:49.891
who shaped the land remain a living
present,

301
00:17:49.891 --> 00:17:51.731
still holding the law in place.

302
00:17:53.571 --> 00:17:56.411
And where is this law written, if not in a
book?

303
00:17:56.711 --> 00:17:59.951
It's written in the land itself, and in
the songs that cross it.

304
00:18:00.251 --> 00:18:03.271
The famous songlines are paths through the
country,

305
00:18:03.311 --> 00:18:07.291
and to sing the song in order is to recite
the features of the landscape in order,

306
00:18:07.291 --> 00:18:09.731
and woven into the same performance are
the law,

307
00:18:09.731 --> 00:18:12.371
the genealogy, the ethics, and the map.

308
00:18:12.911 --> 00:18:15.971
The land is the scripture, and you read it
by walking and singing.

309
00:18:16.791 --> 00:18:21.351
It is dharma as total order, carried
entirely in voice, movement, and place.

310
00:18:23.051 --> 00:18:26.671
You might think something so unwritten
must be vague or quick to drift,

311
00:18:26.711 --> 00:18:28.411
but the evidence says otherwise.

312
00:18:29.011 --> 00:18:30.951
Researchers gathered Aboriginal stories

313
00:18:30.971 --> 00:18:33.611
from twenty-one places around the
Australian coast,

314
00:18:33.691 --> 00:18:37.971
all describing a time when the sea stood
lower, and the shore lay further out.

315
00:18:38.451 --> 00:18:41.091
They are not myths in the dismissive
sense.

316
00:18:41.831 --> 00:18:46.251
On the reading of the geographer Patrick
Nunn and the linguist Nicholas Reid,

317
00:18:46.251 --> 00:18:47.451
who did the matching,

318
00:18:47.611 --> 00:18:49.791
they appear to be memories of the
coastlines

319
00:18:49.791 --> 00:18:52.111
that drowned when the last ice age ended,

320
00:18:52.191 --> 00:18:55.731
carried by mouth, faithfully enough to
still fit the geology,

321
00:18:55.731 --> 00:18:58.551
for something between seven and thirteen
thousand years.

322
00:18:59.151 --> 00:19:00.771
Oral does not mean fragile.

323
00:19:01.451 --> 00:19:04.691
High fidelity across hundreds of
generations turns out

324
00:19:04.691 --> 00:19:07.431
to be an achievement of careful cultural
machinery.

325
00:19:09.211 --> 00:19:13.111
Two features recur across indigenous
traditions well beyond Australia,

326
00:19:13.111 --> 00:19:17.371
each belonging to its own people rather
than to a single indigenous creed.

327
00:19:17.811 --> 00:19:20.311
The first is what some call kincentricity:

328
00:19:20.311 --> 00:19:24.671
the moral circle extends beyond human
beings to encompass the living world,

329
00:19:24.671 --> 00:19:29.371
so that the river, the animal, and the
plant are not resources but relatives,

330
00:19:29.411 --> 00:19:31.951
owed the same reciprocity you owe your
kin.

331
00:19:32.391 --> 00:19:36.571
The botanist Robin Wall Kimmerer, writing
from the Potawatomi tradition,

332
00:19:36.571 --> 00:19:41.151
calls humans "the younger brothers of
Creation," the ones with the most to learn.

333
00:19:41.591 --> 00:19:45.911
The second is the long time horizon: the
Haudenosaunee of North America,

334
00:19:45.911 --> 00:19:48.811
who maintained an entire constitution
orally for centuries,

335
00:19:49.331 --> 00:19:52.771
are famous for the principle that every
serious decision must be weighed

336
00:19:52.771 --> 00:19:55.811
for its effects seven generations
downstream.

337
00:19:56.071 --> 00:20:00.331
Place, kin, and the unborn are all inside
the circle of care.

338
00:20:01.981 --> 00:20:05.191
The scholar Vine Deloria Jr drew a
distinction

339
00:20:05.191 --> 00:20:07.951
that is better used as a lens than as a
filing system:

340
00:20:08.491 --> 00:20:11.231
religions of time and religions of space.

341
00:20:11.771 --> 00:20:13.931
The Axial and Abrahamic traditions,

342
00:20:14.031 --> 00:20:18.091
and arguably the secular outlook as well,
are religions of time.

343
00:20:18.471 --> 00:20:22.211
They tell a universal story that runs from
a beginning toward an end,

344
00:20:22.291 --> 00:20:25.991
and because the story does not depend on
any particular patch of ground,

345
00:20:25.991 --> 00:20:29.851
it can be packed up and carried anywhere,
preached on any continent.

346
00:20:30.211 --> 00:20:32.371
The Dreaming is a religion of space.

347
00:20:33.301 --> 00:20:36.231
It is this land, these rocks, that
waterhole,

348
00:20:36.231 --> 00:20:38.491
and the law is written into the country
itself.

349
00:20:38.931 --> 00:20:40.211
That portability we take

350
00:20:40.211 --> 00:20:42.151
for granted in the great missionary faiths

351
00:20:42.151 --> 00:20:44.911
is not a universal feature of dharmas at
all.

352
00:20:45.491 --> 00:20:47.231
Some of them are bolted to the earth,

353
00:20:47.351 --> 00:20:49.831
and mean nothing the moment you remove
them from it.

354
00:20:51.751 --> 00:20:55.811
It is dangerously easy for outsiders,
especially guilty modern ones,

355
00:20:55.811 --> 00:20:59.851
to turn indigenous peoples into a fantasy
of the noble ecological saint,

356
00:20:59.851 --> 00:21:02.511
the human who lived in perfect harmony
with nature,

357
00:21:02.511 --> 00:21:05.091
as a stick to beat our own civilisation
with.

358
00:21:05.531 --> 00:21:09.851
The anthropologist Shepard Krech showed
how much that image is a projection,

359
00:21:09.851 --> 00:21:12.631
and how indigenous peoples, like all
peoples,

360
00:21:12.631 --> 00:21:15.731
in fact shaped and sometimes depleted
their environments.

361
00:21:16.331 --> 00:21:18.951
The real lesson is more useful than the
fantasy:

362
00:21:18.951 --> 00:21:23.051
these traditions encode genuinely powerful
wisdom about living within limits,

363
00:21:23.151 --> 00:21:25.951
and they do so without their holders
having to be angels.

364
00:21:26.411 --> 00:21:30.111
We can learn from a dharma, as one of its
own custodians warned,

365
00:21:30.191 --> 00:21:34.491
without going around in borrowed feathers,
pretending to own or to become it.

366
00:21:37.521 --> 00:21:39.571
Sumarah: the open hand

367
00:21:41.851 --> 00:21:43.231
Now come back to Java.

368
00:21:43.951 --> 00:21:47.671
The island that gave us the Samin has more
than one dharma to teach,

369
00:21:47.691 --> 00:21:50.551
and the second could hardly be less like
the first.

370
00:21:51.231 --> 00:21:55.751
The Samin met the world with refusal: flat
speech, stubborn plainness,

371
00:21:55.791 --> 00:22:00.251
a wall of polite no. In the court city of
Yogyakarta,

372
00:22:00.291 --> 00:22:05.051
some forty-five years after Samin Surosentiko
began teaching in the teak country,

373
00:22:05.331 --> 00:22:08.211
a quieter movement grew from the opposite
gesture,

374
00:22:08.211 --> 00:22:10.551
and its whole teaching is already in its
name.

375
00:22:11.071 --> 00:22:13.551
Sumarah is Javanese for total surrender.

376
00:22:15.231 --> 00:22:18.671
It began in 1935, when a palace retainer

377
00:22:18.671 --> 00:22:22.271
and bank clerk named Sukinohartono took
the experiences

378
00:22:22.271 --> 00:22:24.951
that came to him in practice to be
revelation.

379
00:22:25.671 --> 00:22:28.571
A circle of friends formed around what he
had received,

380
00:22:28.691 --> 00:22:32.271
grew quietly through the Japanese
occupation and the revolutionary war,

381
00:22:32.271 --> 00:22:36.391
and organised itself around 1950 as
Paguyuban Sumarah,

382
00:22:36.391 --> 00:22:38.411
the fellowship of those who surrender.

383
00:22:38.851 --> 00:22:41.551
What the fellowship does is called sujud.

384
00:22:42.091 --> 00:22:45.731
People sit together, usually weekly and
for hours at a stretch,

385
00:22:45.871 --> 00:22:47.911
in a guided, settling silence;

386
00:22:48.011 --> 00:22:51.591
body, feeling, and finally thinking are
allowed to relax,

387
00:22:51.751 --> 00:22:53.791
attention comes to rest in rasa,

388
00:22:53.891 --> 00:22:57.511
the felt sense that Javanese tradition
treats as an organ of knowing,

389
00:22:57.611 --> 00:23:01.191
and the practitioner surrenders every
aspect of personal being,

390
00:23:01.191 --> 00:23:06.051
in the movement's own formula, "so the self
functions as a vehicle for God's will."

391
00:23:07.551 --> 00:23:11.611
Set this beside the Stoic and the tour
gains its sharpest contrast.

392
00:23:12.511 --> 00:23:16.091
The Stoic drew a line around the one thing
that was truly his,

393
00:23:16.091 --> 00:23:19.891
the quality of his own choices, and built
a fortress on it.

394
00:23:20.251 --> 00:23:22.191
Sumarah gives exactly that away.

395
00:23:22.731 --> 00:23:26.371
Every other dharma we have visited tells
you what to do with your will:

396
00:23:26.511 --> 00:23:31.091
bind it to the community, command it with
reason, walk it along the songline.

397
00:23:32.251 --> 00:23:35.991
This one asks you to open your hand and
let the will itself go.

398
00:23:36.751 --> 00:23:41.211
It is a fifth answer to what a person most
deeply is: not a node in a web,

399
00:23:41.271 --> 00:23:45.471
not a citadel of choice, not a singer of
country, but an instrument.

400
00:23:47.331 --> 00:23:51.211
And it does this with almost none of the
equipment a religion is supposed to need.

401
00:23:51.671 --> 00:23:55.751
There is no scripture; a guide named Pak
Wondo put it flatly:

402
00:23:55.971 --> 00:23:58.231
"In Sumarah we have no sacred book.

403
00:23:58.771 --> 00:24:00.371
Our sacred book is Life."

404
00:24:01.551 --> 00:24:05.551
There are no temples, no clergy, and no
creed to recite.

405
00:24:06.231 --> 00:24:07.911
Above all there is no guru.

406
00:24:08.341 --> 00:24:13.591
The only teacher who finally counts is the
guru sejati, the true teacher within;

407
00:24:13.821 --> 00:24:16.551
the person who guides a session, the
pamong,

408
00:24:16.631 --> 00:24:19.411
is understood not as a master but as a
function,

409
00:24:19.871 --> 00:24:23.651
someone speaking spontaneously out of
attunement to the people in the room,

410
00:24:23.671 --> 00:24:27.191
with an authority that lasts exactly as
long as the attunement does.

411
00:24:27.791 --> 00:24:30.291
Even revelation must pass inspection.

412
00:24:30.991 --> 00:24:35.491
At the fellowship's 1950 congress its
first chairman, Dr Surono,

413
00:24:35.691 --> 00:24:38.831
insisted that guidance claiming the
highest source still had

414
00:24:38.831 --> 00:24:40.531
to be proven before the gathering:

415
00:24:40.851 --> 00:24:44.171
"Even Sukino asks us for our agreement,"
he said,

416
00:24:44.211 --> 00:24:47.851
and then drew the boundary that earns
Sumarah its place on this tour:

417
00:24:48.031 --> 00:24:49.571
"We differ from religions,

418
00:24:49.571 --> 00:24:53.111
within which people accept God on faith
and without knowledge."

419
00:24:54.701 --> 00:24:57.591
Nor, finally, is there anywhere to retreat
to.

420
00:24:58.251 --> 00:25:01.491
The tradition's phrase for its own style
is tapa rame,

421
00:25:01.611 --> 00:25:05.031
asceticism in the crowd: no monastery, no
hermitage,

422
00:25:05.031 --> 00:25:08.691
just the practice carried on inside
ordinary working and family life,

423
00:25:08.711 --> 00:25:10.351
the surrender renewed at the desk

424
00:25:10.351 --> 00:25:13.451
and the market stall as much as in the
meditation hall.

425
00:25:15.111 --> 00:25:17.751
So where does this dharma draw its circle
of care?

426
00:25:18.071 --> 00:25:22.071
At the doctrinal edge, more softly than
any other stop on the tour.

427
00:25:22.731 --> 00:25:24.171
Most members are Muslims,

428
00:25:24.191 --> 00:25:26.751
but Buddhists and Christians sit in the
same silence

429
00:25:26.751 --> 00:25:29.051
without ceasing to be Buddhists and
Christians;

430
00:25:29.391 --> 00:25:31.991
Indonesian researchers studying the
fellowship describe

431
00:25:32.091 --> 00:25:35.511
people of different religions joined in a
single shared ritual,

432
00:25:35.571 --> 00:25:37.751
each remaining inside their own faith.

433
00:25:38.491 --> 00:25:42.311
Almost anyone, it seems, may sit, and
nobody is asked to convert,

434
00:25:42.371 --> 00:25:44.331
because there is nothing to convert to:

435
00:25:44.491 --> 00:25:48.611
no profession of belief is required of
anyone who takes a place in the silence,

436
00:25:48.691 --> 00:25:50.631
and the fellowship has never proselytised.

437
00:25:51.491 --> 00:25:53.511
The hard edge is practical instead.

438
00:25:54.131 --> 00:25:59.711
A practice with no book, no technique, and
no franchise can travel only by contact,

439
00:25:59.811 --> 00:26:02.371
person to person and session to session,

440
00:26:02.571 --> 00:26:05.311
and for most of its history it travelled
in Javanese,

441
00:26:05.571 --> 00:26:09.271
sometimes in the difficult high Javanese
of the founder's own guidance.

442
00:26:10.091 --> 00:26:15.391
Its near-twin Subud, born of the same
Yogyakarta mystical circle in the same decade,

443
00:26:15.451 --> 00:26:18.891
wrapped a portable spiritual exercise in a
uniform format

444
00:26:18.891 --> 00:26:20.751
and carried it around the world;

445
00:26:21.091 --> 00:26:24.131
Sumarah stayed a Javanese fellowship of a
few thousand,

446
00:26:24.131 --> 00:26:29.211
its numbers peaking near ten thousand in
the mid-1970s and drifting down ever since.

447
00:26:29.841 --> 00:26:32.611
Smallness, here, is not a failure of
marketing.

448
00:26:32.911 --> 00:26:36.231
It is the price of refusing every
reproducible form.

449
00:26:38.691 --> 00:26:42.091
And the shadow, because every stop on this
tour has one.

450
00:26:42.751 --> 00:26:45.731
The openness has a floor, and the floor is
God.

451
00:26:46.451 --> 00:26:50.671
When practitioners in Solo developed a
religiously neutral "relaxed meditation"

452
00:26:50.671 --> 00:26:52.151
for officials and foreigners,

453
00:26:52.151 --> 00:26:56.751
the leadership ruled from inside the
practice that meditation without God "led

454
00:26:56.751 --> 00:26:58.331
to void and not God,"

455
00:26:58.351 --> 00:27:01.611
and the national chairman told the Western
practitioners bluntly,

456
00:27:01.611 --> 00:27:03.591
"I stress that you are not members."

457
00:27:05.011 --> 00:27:06.451
That floor has a history,

458
00:27:06.451 --> 00:27:09.491
and the history could not be spoken of
while it was being made.

459
00:27:10.091 --> 00:27:14.211
After the coup of 1965, in the massacres
that followed,

460
00:27:14.231 --> 00:27:15.751
somewhere between half a million

461
00:27:15.751 --> 00:27:19.411
and a million Indonesians suspected of
communism were killed,

462
00:27:19.451 --> 00:27:22.831
and atheism itself, though no statute ever
named it,

463
00:27:22.831 --> 00:27:25.031
was treated as communism's twin:

464
00:27:25.231 --> 00:27:28.771
a citizen who professed no God was marked
as subversive,

465
00:27:28.771 --> 00:27:31.531
and the mystical movements were told, in
effect,

466
00:27:31.531 --> 00:27:34.091
that they could remain open exactly
insofar

467
00:27:34.091 --> 00:27:37.191
as they put surrender to God at the front
of what they were.

468
00:27:37.811 --> 00:27:40.071
Sumarah's God was not invented then;

469
00:27:40.071 --> 00:27:44.971
he is there in the founding vows of 1940,
a generation before the terror.

470
00:27:45.531 --> 00:27:48.331
And a further caution comes from the
language itself:

471
00:27:48.511 --> 00:27:52.131
Javanese and Indonesian public speech is
densely formulaic,

472
00:27:52.211 --> 00:27:56.791
and a talk that opens by acknowledging the
One Almighty God is observing etiquette,

473
00:27:56.951 --> 00:27:59.071
not necessarily making theology.

474
00:27:59.701 --> 00:28:05.011
But from 1965 no one inside the movement
could safely question that God,

475
00:28:05.011 --> 00:28:08.811
and no scholar who cared for the movement
could ask the question in print,

476
00:28:08.831 --> 00:28:12.771
so how much of the policed theism was
devotion, how much etiquette,

477
00:28:12.771 --> 00:28:15.951
and how much armour is something the
record can no longer tell us.

478
00:28:16.551 --> 00:28:18.111
It could not be said then.

479
00:28:18.511 --> 00:28:20.231
At this distance, it can.

480
00:28:21.871 --> 00:28:24.131
A fellowship that tests truth by
collective

481
00:28:24.131 --> 00:28:26.711
feeling has a characteristic failure mode
as well,

482
00:28:26.951 --> 00:28:28.991
and its own historian records it:

483
00:28:29.111 --> 00:28:33.651
where everyone believes there ought to be
unanimity, criticism is quietly repressed.

484
00:28:34.271 --> 00:28:37.191
And that historian is himself the final
caution.

485
00:28:37.851 --> 00:28:40.331
Nearly everything the English-speaking
world knows

486
00:28:40.331 --> 00:28:44.311
about Sumarah passes through one man, Paul
Stange,

487
00:28:44.411 --> 00:28:49.451
a scholar who has also been a practitioner
since 1971, and the teacher who,

488
00:28:49.491 --> 00:28:53.531
as the preface records, first put this
book's question in my path.

489
00:28:54.051 --> 00:28:57.551
I am not a neutral witness here, and
neither are my sources.

490
00:28:58.191 --> 00:29:00.651
Whether a practice like this could give up
its God

491
00:29:00.651 --> 00:29:03.511
and still be itself is a question held
over,

492
00:29:03.511 --> 00:29:05.491
at full length, for Part 7.

493
00:29:08.401 --> 00:29:10.451
The Covenant: looking at your own

494
00:29:12.751 --> 00:29:14.811
For most readers in the Western world,

495
00:29:14.811 --> 00:29:17.371
the tradition you absorbed before you
could choose,

496
00:29:17.371 --> 00:29:19.451
whether or not you still believe a word of
it,

497
00:29:19.451 --> 00:29:22.671
is one of the three faiths that trace
themselves to Abraham.

498
00:29:23.211 --> 00:29:25.591
And the reflex bred into the modern mind
is

499
00:29:25.591 --> 00:29:28.891
to file these under a different and
opposite heading from "dharma":

500
00:29:28.891 --> 00:29:32.751
under religion, the thing of belief and
faith and the supernatural,

501
00:29:32.751 --> 00:29:36.051
as against ethics or philosophy or a way
of life.

502
00:29:37.111 --> 00:29:38.731
That reflex is mistaken,

503
00:29:38.851 --> 00:29:42.451
and I should own that the bookshelf behind
this project shares its tilt --

504
00:29:42.631 --> 00:29:46.911
heavy on evolution and secular philosophy,
light on the faiths of Abraham --

505
00:29:47.111 --> 00:29:50.491
which is exactly why this stop on the tour
gets its full weight.

506
00:29:50.971 --> 00:29:53.931
Judaism, Christianity, and Islam are,

507
00:29:53.931 --> 00:29:57.191
by every test we have used, dharmas like
any other.

508
00:29:58.771 --> 00:30:01.791
The clinching evidence is sitting in their
own vocabulary.

509
00:30:02.071 --> 00:30:05.491
Halakha, the word for the whole body of
Jewish practice,

510
00:30:05.531 --> 00:30:07.511
comes from a root meaning "to walk,"

511
00:30:07.731 --> 00:30:11.591
and it is best translated not as "law" but
as "the way to walk."

512
00:30:11.951 --> 00:30:16.191
Sharia, in Islam, literally means "the
path to water."

513
00:30:16.411 --> 00:30:19.911
And the very first Christians, before
anyone called them Christians,

514
00:30:19.911 --> 00:30:22.171
called themselves followers of "the Way."

515
00:30:22.251 --> 00:30:25.231
The same image in all three: a path one
walks.

516
00:30:25.831 --> 00:30:29.391
That is precisely the image buried in the
word dharma itself,

517
00:30:29.691 --> 00:30:33.611
and in its cousin mārga, the Sanskrit word
for a road or path.

518
00:30:34.131 --> 00:30:36.211
These traditions describe themselves,

519
00:30:36.231 --> 00:30:39.771
in their own oldest language, exactly as a
dharma does.

520
00:30:41.571 --> 00:30:44.151
And the path-image goes deeper than
etymology.

521
00:30:44.711 --> 00:30:47.631
The modern assumption that religion is
essentially belief,

522
00:30:47.791 --> 00:30:50.211
a set of propositions you hold in your
head,

523
00:30:50.251 --> 00:30:54.911
is a peculiar and recent one, shaped
largely by Protestant Christianity.

524
00:30:55.391 --> 00:30:57.831
Judaism and Islam do not fit it well at
all,

525
00:30:57.831 --> 00:31:00.411
because they are overwhelmingly traditions
of practice:

526
00:31:00.871 --> 00:31:04.451
comprehensive orders covering food, law,
marriage,

527
00:31:04.571 --> 00:31:07.791
money, the shape of the day and the shape
of the year.

528
00:31:08.191 --> 00:31:10.231
When an observant Jew keeps kosher,

529
00:31:10.271 --> 00:31:13.831
or a Muslim pauses to pray five times
between dawn and night,

530
00:31:14.011 --> 00:31:18.731
what is mainly happening is not a proposition
being affirmed but a day being shaped.

531
00:31:19.271 --> 00:31:23.771
And for most of its history Christianity
did not fit the belief-picture either:

532
00:31:23.771 --> 00:31:26.911
the liturgy and the sacraments, the
fasting calendar,

533
00:31:26.911 --> 00:31:29.611
confession, the monastic round of hours,

534
00:31:29.631 --> 00:31:33.851
made a Christian life a shaped day long
before it was a list of affirmations.

535
00:31:34.391 --> 00:31:39.451
The belief-first picture is the Protestant
exception, mistaken for the family rule.

536
00:31:39.871 --> 00:31:43.771
A shaped day is the very definition of a
dharma as we built it.

537
00:31:44.211 --> 00:31:47.691
In this sense the great Abrahamic faiths
are more dharma-like

538
00:31:47.691 --> 00:31:50.331
than the "religion equals belief" picture
allows.

539
00:31:51.991 --> 00:31:56.011
What genuinely sets them apart is the kind
of ground they stand on.

540
00:31:56.391 --> 00:31:57.771
The Indic, Chinese,

541
00:31:57.771 --> 00:32:01.091
and indigenous dharmas tend to align you
with an impersonal order,

542
00:32:01.091 --> 00:32:04.411
the ṛta or the Tao or the law in the land.

543
00:32:04.771 --> 00:32:09.491
The Abrahamic dharmas instead rest on a
covenant: a binding, personal,

544
00:32:09.571 --> 00:32:13.071
historical relationship between a people
and a God who commands,

545
00:32:13.071 --> 00:32:14.791
promises, and remembers.

546
00:32:15.311 --> 00:32:19.151
The order is not a structure to be
discerned but a Person to be answered.

547
00:32:19.811 --> 00:32:22.431
And where does this dharma draw its circle
of care?

548
00:32:23.281 --> 00:32:27.971
A covenant binds a particular people, a
chosen "us" with a definite edge,

549
00:32:28.311 --> 00:32:31.831
even as Christianity and Islam extend the
same logic outward,

550
00:32:31.891 --> 00:32:34.791
offering the covenant to anyone willing to
walk its path.

551
00:32:35.911 --> 00:32:39.411
That is a real difference, and it brings
real consequences,

552
00:32:39.551 --> 00:32:42.651
including the hard question we have
already met of whether ethics

553
00:32:42.651 --> 00:32:46.231
that rests on God's command can outlive
belief in that God.

554
00:32:48.021 --> 00:32:50.791
Two common stereotypes deserve puncturing.

555
00:32:51.531 --> 00:32:56.811
Islam carries inside its own law a list of
the law's higher purposes, the maqasid,

556
00:32:56.811 --> 00:33:00.871
which the medieval thinker al-Ghazali
summed up as the protection of religion,

557
00:33:00.951 --> 00:33:03.811
life, intellect, lineage, and property.

558
00:33:04.391 --> 00:33:09.271
That is a floor of protected human goods,
built into the tradition centuries ago,

559
00:33:09.271 --> 00:33:12.611
and it looks remarkably like the lists of
basic human capabilities

560
00:33:12.611 --> 00:33:16.771
that modern secular philosophers have
proposed as a universal minimum.

561
00:33:17.331 --> 00:33:19.931
And Christianity contains two strands in
tension.

562
00:33:20.871 --> 00:33:24.891
One, the Pauline strand, makes love the
whole of ethics,

563
00:33:25.151 --> 00:33:28.391
declaring that "love is the fulfilling of
the law," and can seem

564
00:33:28.391 --> 00:33:32.511
to dissolve detailed path-walking into a
single inner disposition.

565
00:33:33.151 --> 00:33:35.071
But alongside it runs another,

566
00:33:35.151 --> 00:33:36.351
the natural-law tradition

567
00:33:36.351 --> 00:33:40.391
that Thomas Aquinas built by marrying
Christian faith to Aristotle,

568
00:33:40.431 --> 00:33:42.571
in which the core of morality is available

569
00:33:42.571 --> 00:33:45.351
to human reason reflecting on human
nature,

570
00:33:45.411 --> 00:33:47.811
with no special revelation required.

571
00:33:48.411 --> 00:33:53.531
That strand is the direct ancestor of the
secular, nature-grounded ethics I lean on.

572
00:33:54.231 --> 00:33:57.091
The supposed opposite of secular dharma
turns out

573
00:33:57.091 --> 00:33:58.911
to contain one of its grandparents.

574
00:34:00.911 --> 00:34:03.271
And these are living traditions, not
fossils,

575
00:34:03.271 --> 00:34:06.031
which is what the secular eye most easily
misses.

576
00:34:06.631 --> 00:34:09.811
The very maqasid, the higher purposes of
the law,

577
00:34:09.831 --> 00:34:12.671
are what reformers reach for when they
argue that a cruel

578
00:34:12.671 --> 00:34:16.091
or patriarchal reading has lost sight of
what the law was for:

579
00:34:16.471 --> 00:34:18.891
movements such as Sisters in Islam contest

580
00:34:19.111 --> 00:34:21.231
extremist interpretations by reaching
further

581
00:34:21.231 --> 00:34:23.911
into the tradition rather than walking
away from it.

582
00:34:24.631 --> 00:34:28.691
Both Islam and Judaism carry centuries of
fierce internal argument,

583
00:34:28.751 --> 00:34:30.631
and both shelter mystical,

584
00:34:30.711 --> 00:34:33.511
inward streams, Sufism and Kabbalah,

585
00:34:33.551 --> 00:34:36.231
every bit as contemplative as anything in
Buddhism.

586
00:34:36.771 --> 00:34:39.971
A dharma is not its most rigid and
frightened version.

587
00:34:40.471 --> 00:34:42.951
It is a quiet failure of the
non-privileging eye

588
00:34:42.951 --> 00:34:45.891
to define other people's traditions by
their fundamentalists

589
00:34:45.891 --> 00:34:47.911
while defining our own by their
philosophers.

590
00:34:49.931 --> 00:34:54.051
If the Abrahamic paths are dharmas, then
so is whatever I am doing here.

591
00:34:54.831 --> 00:34:57.411
"Secular dharma" is not a neutral throne
above the others

592
00:34:57.411 --> 00:34:59.071
from which I get to rank them.

593
00:34:59.551 --> 00:35:01.691
It is one more path among the paths,

594
00:35:01.691 --> 00:35:04.491
with its own commitments and its own blind
spots,

595
00:35:04.751 --> 00:35:08.331
walking alongside the covenant, the
Dreaming, and the rest.

596
00:35:08.951 --> 00:35:13.671
The non-privileging stance, taken
seriously, privileges me least of all.

597
00:35:16.771 --> 00:35:17.591
What rhymes

598
00:35:19.961 --> 00:35:23.451
So we have our five, and they really are
profoundly different.

599
00:35:24.131 --> 00:35:28.291
Set them side by side and they disagree
about the deepest things there are.

600
00:35:29.211 --> 00:35:33.571
They disagree about what a person even is:
a node in a living web,

601
00:35:33.751 --> 00:35:38.631
a self-contained citadel of choice, or an
instrument surrendered to its God.

602
00:35:39.051 --> 00:35:43.171
They disagree about time: a line running
from creation to judgement,

603
00:35:43.391 --> 00:35:47.511
or an "everywhen" in which the ancestral
past is permanently present.

604
00:35:47.991 --> 00:35:52.431
They disagree about the ground of it all:
an impersonal order you align with,

605
00:35:52.451 --> 00:35:57.811
a commanding God you answer to, a silent
God you yield to, a land you belong to.

606
00:35:58.171 --> 00:36:02.531
This diversity does not melt away on
inspection; it goes all the way down.

607
00:36:02.951 --> 00:36:06.011
The claim that the world's wisdom
traditions are "all really saying

608
00:36:06.011 --> 00:36:09.971
the same thing" does not survive a hard
look at what they actually say.

609
00:36:11.521 --> 00:36:13.771
And yet, if you stand back far enough,

610
00:36:13.771 --> 00:36:16.971
the family resemblance is just as
undeniable as the difference.

611
00:36:18.131 --> 00:36:22.011
Every one of these dharmas, each in the
form we have met it here,

612
00:36:22.191 --> 00:36:24.911
carries some version of the oldest rule we
know,

613
00:36:24.971 --> 00:36:27.611
the call to treat others as you would be
treated,

614
00:36:27.791 --> 00:36:29.731
whether it is extended to the village,

615
00:36:29.771 --> 00:36:34.431
the cosmopolis, the more-than-human world,
or the covenant community.

616
00:36:34.851 --> 00:36:38.451
Every one of them binds an "us," turning a
crowd of strangers

617
00:36:38.451 --> 00:36:40.751
into a people who can trust one another,

618
00:36:40.751 --> 00:36:45.331
and every one draws that circle with a
hard edge as well as a warm inside.

619
00:36:45.751 --> 00:36:48.191
Every one of them forms character through
practice

620
00:36:48.191 --> 00:36:50.891
and habit rather than through assent
alone.

621
00:36:51.271 --> 00:36:53.391
And every one of them treats the moral
order

622
00:36:53.391 --> 00:36:56.431
as continuous with the order of reality
itself,

623
00:36:56.571 --> 00:37:00.631
so that to live rightly is to come into
tune with the way things truly are.

624
00:37:02.551 --> 00:37:04.511
That is the shape we keep arriving at.

625
00:37:05.091 --> 00:37:08.671
The variety is real, thick, and not to be
flattened,

626
00:37:08.691 --> 00:37:11.751
and underneath it runs a thin band of
shared human concern

627
00:37:11.751 --> 00:37:14.371
that no functioning dharma seems to do
without.

628
00:37:14.951 --> 00:37:19.651
Difference all the way down, and kinship
all the way down, at the same time.

629
00:37:20.051 --> 00:37:24.371
One honesty is owed here: these five were
chosen because they rhyme,

630
00:37:24.411 --> 00:37:28.611
so this gallery illustrates the family
resemblance; it cannot prove it.

631
00:37:28.851 --> 00:37:30.171
Later, when we have

632
00:37:30.171 --> 00:37:34.331
to ask the dangerous question of whether
some dharmas are simply better than others,

633
00:37:34.551 --> 00:37:38.551
that thin shared band is going to be the
only honest place to stand.

634
00:37:41.651 --> 00:37:42.971
Made of the same need

635
00:37:45.251 --> 00:37:50.251
Taken whole, the world of dharmas shows
human beings facing the one problem of how

636
00:37:50.251 --> 00:37:51.651
to live well together,

637
00:37:51.691 --> 00:37:53.771
and building answers of breathtaking
variety.

638
00:37:55.231 --> 00:38:00.231
A relational web in southern Africa, a
fortress of the will in imperial Rome,

639
00:38:00.231 --> 00:38:03.111
a sung and walked law in the Australian
desert,

640
00:38:03.271 --> 00:38:09.271
an open hand in Java, a covenant in the
Near East: five solutions to one need,

641
00:38:09.291 --> 00:38:13.831
each fully itself, each unmistakably a
member of the same family.

642
00:38:15.411 --> 00:38:18.551
But I have been describing these dharmas
from the outside,

643
00:38:18.671 --> 00:38:22.511
as an observer strolling through a
gallery, comparing the exhibits.

644
00:38:22.991 --> 00:38:25.811
That is not how anyone actually lives
inside one.

645
00:38:26.711 --> 00:38:30.911
From the inside, your dharma does not feel
like one option among five.

646
00:38:31.951 --> 00:38:35.271
It does not feel chosen, or built, or
cultural at all.

647
00:38:35.791 --> 00:38:39.871
It feels like simply the truth, like the
plain shape of reality,

648
00:38:39.971 --> 00:38:44.591
as obvious and unremarkable as the ground
being solid and the sky being up.

649
00:38:44.991 --> 00:38:49.271
The elder does not experience "a
relational ontology of personhood."

650
00:38:49.271 --> 00:38:53.211
She experiences the plain fact that a
person needs other people.

651
00:38:53.771 --> 00:38:58.451
That is the real power of a working
dharma, and it is also its deepest mystery.

652
00:38:58.971 --> 00:39:03.371
How does a made thing get so far inside a
human being that it stops feeling made?

653
00:39:03.911 --> 00:39:06.331
How does a path you were simply born onto
come

654
00:39:06.331 --> 00:39:08.351
to feel like the only ground there is?

655
00:39:10.201 --> 00:39:13.151
To answer that, we have to stop looking at
what dharmas say

656
00:39:13.431 --> 00:39:16.131
and start looking at what they do to the
people who hold them,

657
00:39:16.391 --> 00:39:19.111
how they get under the skin, into the
habits,

658
00:39:19.351 --> 00:39:21.551
and down into the nervous system itself.

659
00:39:22.011 --> 00:39:25.551
We have to ask not what a dharma teaches,
but how it installs.

